{
  // THE RUST WORKER'S CANARY, and this file is why a cutover is a one-line change instead of a
  // decision made in a hurry.
  //
  // `wrangler.jsonc` names `src/index.js` and deploys the JavaScript. This one names
  // `worker/build/index.js` — the module `worker-build --release` produced, the one
  // `worker/verify.mjs` runs against the shipping JavaScript on thirteen cases — and binds THE SAME
  // assets directory and the SAME R2 bucket under a DIFFERENT worker name, so the two can be smoke
  // side by side and neither one is a guess about the other.
  //
  // WHY A CANARY AND NOT A CUTOVER: the harness proves the bytes, and the bytes are the thing that
  // was uncertain. What the harness CANNOT prove is the two bindings' real behaviour — `R2Object`
  // answers `size`/`httpEtag` as properties on a real object, and the ASSETS pass-through preserves
  // range and conditional headers the stub never saw. A deploy that answers the same bytes under the
  // real bindings is the last unknown removed, and it is removed somewhere that is not the URL every
  // device's `setup` installs from.
  //
  // `workers_dev: true` and no custom domain, deliberately: the production name carries a domain
  // that Cloudflare ignores in this config, so the canary is reachable only on its workers.dev URL.
  //
  // ── THE SMOKE RAN, AND IT FOUND A DEFECT NO HARNESS COULD SEE ─────────────────────────────────
  //
  // `*.workers.dev` is unreachable from both machines this loop runs on (this box: a TLS reset, the
  // same one `scripts/build.sh` records for `wrangler tail`; the device: `CANARY 000 0` while
  // `agent.saisi.online` answers 200 from the same shell in the same second) — hence the zone route
  // and the `agent-rust` CNAME, both of which are REMOVABLE: delete the record and the route.
  //
  // **WHAT THE CANARY PROVED, on 2026-09-29, against `agent.saisi.online` itself:** all eight routes
  // byte-identical once the host is normalised — the landing page on both paths, the manifest, the
  // 404s, the tarball, cloudflared, and BOTH R2 artifacts at their full size. The host is the one
  // difference, and it is the difference the worker is SUPPOSED to have: every URL is rebuilt against
  // the request's own origin, so a manifest cannot point a device at another host.
  //
  // **AND THE ONE DEFECT THAT ONLY THE LIVE RUN COULD FIND.** `ObjectBody::stream()` reads the R2
  // object THROUGH THE ISOLATE; `ObjectBody::response_body()` hands the raw stream to the runtime,
  // which is what `new Response(obj.body, …)` does in the JavaScript. On the 115 MB electron runtime
  // the first one arrived SHORT on every fetch (21.6 / 71.8 / 76.5 / 80.6 MB of 115,028,145) and the
  // second delivered the full object in 34–54 s — production's own figure is 52.5 s. **A 32 MB object
  // completed either way, so the 13 verify cases, the eight-byte stub, and every local measurement were
  // all green while a 115 MB download was quietly broken.** That is the argument for deploying to a
  // name before a name everyone installs from.
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "summrise-dist-rust",
  "main": "worker/build/index.js",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-01",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
  "workers_dev": true,
  // AND A NAME ON THE ZONE, BECAUSE `*.workers.dev` IS UNREACHABLE FROM BOTH MACHINES (measured
  // above, and the file says so twice because it is the reason this block exists).
  //
  // `agent-rust.saisi.online` is a NEW hostname that answers nothing today, on the same zone the
  // production download host is on — so the canary becomes reachable from this box and from the
  // device, and a byte comparison against `agent.saisi.online` is possible at last. **The production
  // name is NOT in this file and is not touched by this deploy**: the route is additive, and removing
  // it is a second one-line change. (The token carries Workers Routes write — the repo's own
  // `zen-us` worker deploys the same way — and if that stops being true the deploy fails loudly here
  // rather than silently leaving the canary unreachable.)
  "routes": [
    { "pattern": "agent-rust.saisi.online/*", "zone_id": "79860b60a8681147d0ce38294d404ead" }
  ],
  // THE SAME ASSETS DIRECTORY AS PRODUCTION, and that is what makes the comparison mean anything:
  // the canary serves the real release artifacts, the real version.json and the real landing page,
  // so a byte difference between the two workers cannot be an artifact difference.
  "assets": { "directory": "./public", "binding": "ASSETS" },
  "r2_buckets": [{ "binding": "TEMP_FILES", "bucket_name": "summrise-temp-files" }],
  "observability": { "enabled": false }
}
